*Context: *Developed by University of Massachusetts Amherst Professor Emery
Berger*, CSRankings
<https://substack.com/redirect/fb1e2de0-b2f1-4f8c-bad1-196234989a10?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
has emerged as an influential ranking system to identify the top
universities in computer science, including the subfields of AI, systems,
theory , and interdisciplinary areas (e.g., human-computer interaction).
When I give presentations on China=E2=80=99s AI development, my slides on C=
hinese
AI talent draw on CSRankings metrics. Thus, I was intrigued to see that
Chinese science and tech media are closely tracking the latest CSRankings
updates, as reflected in this week=E2=80=99s feature translation (link to *=
original
xinzhiyuan [AI_Era] post
<https://substack.com/redirect/420a7075-a7a6-4403-b366-bea4585538cb?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
).

The reason I use CSRankings in my own research is because it avoids two
issues that beset other ranking systems: 1) unlike US News and World
Report, CS Rankings doesn=E2=80=99t rely on subjective surveys that may be =
too
beholden to past reputations rather than present-day performance; 2)
citation-based metrics can be manipulated by universities and =E2=80=9C*cit=
ation
cartels
<https://substack.com/redirect/e666a600-c5e2-45c3-ae4f-a5f6c5435273?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*=E2=80=9D.
Instead, CSRankings simply judges departments *based
<https://substack.com/redirect/46aba563-ddd5-4900-a3e6-69e42ce9a88d?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
on their faculty=E2=80=99s publications at selective computer science confe=
rences.

*Key Takeaways: AI_Era highlights that Tsinghua University has taken the
top spot in the 2025 CSRankings for the first time, overtaking Carnegie
Mellon University (*see screenshot below).

   -

   Tsinghua University, Shanghai Jiao Tong University (my dad=E2=80=99s alm=
a
   mater), Zhejiang University, and Peking University ranked 1st, 3rd, 4th,
   and 5th, which means Chinese universities had four of the top five ranks
   -

   From the article: =E2=80=9CWith Tsinghua University reaching the top spo=
t,
   China=E2=80=99s global =E2=80=98discursive power=E2=80=99 [=E8=AF=9D=E8=
=AF=AD=E6=9D=83] in computer science has
   significantly increased.=E2=80=9D In terms of this quiet shift in the gl=
obal
   landscape of computer science academia, AI_Era concludes, =E2=80=9C*Expe=
rts
   predict that if Chinese universities continue to focus on open-source
   ecosystems, international research collaborations, and cultivating young
   talent, achieving half of the top 10 spots in the CS Rankings within the
   next five years is =E2=80=98not an unattainable goal*.=E2=80=99=E2=80=9D

<https://substack.com/redirect/f34756ea-7be8-465d-a5e5-8673db284d2e?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>
Source:
CSRankings (all fields on, 2024 to 2025, institutions from around the world=
)

*If we zoom into the AI subcategory of the CSRankings, however, a different
picture emerges. *

   -

   The AI_Era article claims that Peking University ranks No. 1 globally in
   the AI category =E2=80=94 based on just the year 2025 and all the CSRank=
ings AI
   conferences in computer vision, natural language processing, and
   information retrieval, etc. *Here=E2=80=99s
   <https://substack.com/redirect/5eb69e20-f900-40d0-9a4d-02ac18d1f34e?j=3D=
eyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
   how they sorted things.
   -

   If I were to pick the best indicator of the strongest AI universities
   around the world, I would instead use the three most selective machine
   learning conferences (ICLR, ICML, and NeurIPS) and use data from the las=
t
   five years, as one-year snapshots can be distorting. For my results, see
   image below.

<https://substack.com/redirect/84297db5-257a-4d92-a5b7-9f9c6b113aa2?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>
Jeff=E2=80=99s
query on CSrankings.org

   -

   Based on this methodology, Chinese universities are certainly impressive
   ,with three of the usual suspects ranking in the top 10 (Peking Universi=
ty,
   Tsinghua University, and Shanghai Jiao Tong University).
   -

   Still, overall, CMU, MIT, and UC Berkeley are at the top, and US
   universities hold 13 of the top 20 slots. In my opinion, this gives a
   better picture of the world=E2=80=99s top 20 AI universities than the AI=
_Era query.

*I=E2=80=99ll conclude with a few more musings on the influence of CSRankin=
gs, as
it=E2=80=99s remarkable that smart Chinese tech reporters are following it =
so
closely. *Cynthia Rudin, a Duke University Professor who leads an
Interpretable Machine Learning Lab, wrote up a fascinating post that
pinpoints some of the *flaws
<https://substack.com/redirect/8eabb431-e7d5-4ba1-9340-87a7f4f3b730?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
in this rankings system. A few details from her post that stuck out to me:

   -

   Rudin points out that CSRankings may capture a lot of low-quality
   papers: =E2=80=9C*All conferences accept papers of widely varying conten=
t,
   quality, and topic. The review process at these conferences is fast and
   sloppy, and reviewers often have little experience or are from a differe=
nt
   subfield than the paper they are reviewing. Many papers at the conferenc=
es
   are actually not particularly interesting or revolutionary - even readin=
g
   the titles of the papers would allow you to understand this - and many
   other papers are just =E2=80=98click bait=E2=80=99 papers that generate =
attention from
   reviewers on hot topics but have little insight, and no new important
   methods or applications=E2=80=A6CSRankings encourages this fast and slop=
py way of
   doing science to get as many papers as possible into these few venues*.=
=E2=80=9D
   -

   Rudin=E2=80=99s post also mentions that CSRankings is shaping the comput=
er
   science academic job market: =E2=80=9C*I heard recently about someone be=
ing
   hired for a faculty position (thankfully not at Duke!) so the department
   could count their copious low-ish quality papers for CSRankings. This is
   too disgusting for me to comment on it further*.=E2=80=9D

